course: dictionary
lesson: 7903
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them tell you which one the writer means. The word rash can be a noun, a patch of red spots on the skin, or an adjective, meaning acting too quickly without thinking. «The hairs of some caterpillars cause rashes, so you do not touch hairy caterpillars» is about spots on the skin.
explain: Look at the words next to rash. In «a rash decision», the word decision is the clue: a rash decision is one made too fast, without thinking about what might happen. In «a rash of break-ins», the word of tells you that rash here means many of something in a short time.
explain: When you meet a word with more than one meaning, try each meaning in the sentence and keep the one that makes sense. This is the same context check you used before, but now you are choosing between two or three meanings instead of guessing one.
fact: Rash can be a noun: an area of red spots, bumps or blotches on the skin, often itchy.
fact: Rash can be an adjective: «a rash decision» is a decision made too quickly, without thinking.
fact: «A rash of break-ins» means many break-ins in a short time.
fact: Rashes can come from heat, insect bites, allergies or illnesses such as chickenpox; most are harmless, but tell an adult about any rash.
fact: A rash that does not fade when you press a glass on it needs urgent medical help.
exercise: «The hairs of some caterpillars cause rashes, so you do not touch hairy caterpillars.» What are the rashes here? | Fast, careless choices | Thick, dark clouds | *Red, itchy spots or bumps on the skin
exercise: In «a rash decision», what does rash mean? | Covered in red spots | Slow and very careful | *Too quick, made without thinking first
exercise: In «a rash of break-ins», what does rash mean? | A small red spot on the skin | Only one, carefully planned | *Many of them in a short time
test: «Poison ivy gives an itchy, blistering rash.» What is the rash here? | A quick decision made without thinking | Many break-ins in one week | *An area of red, blistering spots on the skin
test: A rash does not fade when you press a glass on it. What should you do? | *Tell an adult and get urgent medical help | Ignore it, it is probably harmless | Wash it and wait a day to see
exam: Which sentence uses rash as an adjective? | *Don't be rash, check first before you jump in | A heat rash on the arm | An itchy rash after a new medicine | A rash of spots on the back
